I’ll first inspect the public pattern/metadata and the framework’s documented analysis tools, then build and compare chemically plausible phase models. I’ll keep structural coordinates fixed until the inventory and profile model are stable, and I’ll validate the final GSAS-II project by reopening/replaying it before writing the deliverables.

The chemistry points first to Ca–W–O products plus residual CaO/WO₃, but I’m treating that only as a candidate inventory. I’ll now extract peak families directly from the scan and query the frozen public structure index for Ca/W/O entries and chemically sensible analogues, including ordered multication parents that can collapse onto Ca sites.

The peak families now support two assignments strongly: scheelite CaWO₄ and monoclinic WO₃. The WO₃ triplet near 23–24.3° and its repeated split families through 60° match a passing P2₁/c source in a different setting; higher-symmetry WO₃ alternatives miss those splittings. I’m testing whether the weaker remaining family is truly CaO or a Ca-rich tungstate before adding it.

The first two-phase Rietveld model is refining now. Its coarse starting fit already required both phases with positive scales; the next pass is resolving cells, angular correction, and peak-shape terms so that any unmodeled residual family can be judged without confusing it with profile mismatch.

The remaining residuals form a coherent Ca-rich tungstate family (not CaO): peaks near 19.36, 22.04, 30.66, 37.45, 45.14, 50.72, 65.79, and 74.86° track a contracted A₂BWO₆-type cell. I’ve built physically screened Ca₃WO₆ analogues from both ordered double-perovskite parents and the composition-nearest Ca₃UO₆ parent; I’m comparing them under identical low-flexibility conditions before allowing the profile to re-equilibrate.

The first usable matched comparisons confirm a third crystalline phase at roughly 5–6 wt% of the modeled crystalline material. The exact-parent ordering variants are close enough that the quick ranking is not decisive; I’m restaging the failed imports and will only choose after full nuisance/profile restarts, as requested.

The crystalline inventory is now strongly three-phase: scheelite CaWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and a Ca-rich Ca₃WO₆-type phase. I’m now completing the profile-mechanism tests and matched analogue swaps; the third phase’s exact ordered framework is the main remaining structural ambiguity.

The current residual is dominated by systematic peak-position and intensity families, not unexplained extra lines. I’m comparing zero offset, specimen displacement, and their joint model from independent starts before allowing texture or atomic parameters to absorb those errors.

The first angular comparison is reproducible: two zero-offset starts converge to the same curve and fractions (~75.1/14.6/10.4 wt%). A displacement-only branch is slightly better, so I’m finishing its restart and the correlated joint branch before choosing the correction.

Background order is a real but modest effect: low-order and alternate cosine forms underfit the broad baseline, while 16–20 Chebyshev terms remain positive and reduce Rwp without moving the angular correction. I’m also screening physically motivated Q-series backgrounds to ensure the polynomial family is not winning by flexibility alone.

Surface roughness is decisively supported: three physical starts converge to nearly the same low-angle attenuation curve and stable fractions, dropping Rwp from 12.30% to about 10.11%. The individual roughness coefficients remain highly correlated, so I’m judging that pair by its reproducible forward correction, as required, and will continue it through later restarts.

Preferred orientation is phase-specific rather than a generic intensity patch: CaWO₄ axes refine essentially to random, while WO₃ shows a strong, coherent [011] response and the Ca₃WO₆-type phase a weaker [100] response. I’m now restarting those candidates jointly with roughness and all retained profile terms active; failed cubic-axis screens are being treated as inconclusive, not evidence against texture.

The retained broadening model is being sharpened: a positive high-angle Gaussian term is reproducible, while the common Lorentzian X branch immediately crosses into negative, clipped widths and is being rejected. The U/V pair is mathematically positive but highly correlated, so it will only survive if the full restart preserves cells, fractions, and the curve.

The phase-broadening screen is separating mechanisms cleanly: CaWO₄ strain attempts go negative or singular, while mild uniaxial size along c is physical; WO₃ isotropic strain is positive but only marginal. I’m finishing the remaining WO₃ and Ca₃WO₆ axes before any term is retained.

The profile pass is complete enough to make structural swaps meaningful. I’m now replacing the Ca₃WO₆-type phase with five provenance-tracked frameworks—cubic, tetragonal, Ca₃UO₆-derived, and two distinct P2₁/n double-perovskite parents—and fully restarting each under the same roughness, displacement, width, size, and WO₃-texture model.

The matched source-swap refinements are still running. The cubic and tetragonal Ca₃WO₆ analogues are essentially tied so far; I’m carrying the lower-symmetry chemistry/topology-nearest parents through the same full nuisance model before deciding.

The P2₁ Ca₃UO₆-derived analogue has now overtaken both higher-symmetry models (Rwp 8.28% versus about 9.06%) with a stable positive fraction near 11.1%. Its fit is not yet final evidence—the refinement still shows a large coordinate-shift uncertainty and it must survive matched broadening/restaging and physical checks.

All five chemistry/topology-nearest parents have completed the same baseline comparison. The two P2₁/n double-perovskite parents remain poor even after restart (Rwp ≈10.8%, weak/unstable minor fractions), while the Ca₃UO₆-derived P2₁ framework gives the only substantial improvement. I’m now testing whether that advantage survives source-specific size/strain mechanisms and independent starts.

The mechanism screen is intentionally matched across all five parents: candidate-only isotropic/axial strain, axial crystallite size, and March–Dollase texture from both sides of unity, with the established background/instrument/sample model held fixed. This will distinguish a genuinely better framework from one that wins only by borrowing a particular broadening or texture term.

Several uniaxial-strain trials are becoming singular or simply returning their starting values, so those branches will be treated as inconclusive—not evidence against a parent. The size and texture branches are converging normally; any promising branch will be restaged from low flexibility and rerun with all retained nuisance terms free.

A strong, reproducible signal has emerged for the P2₁ parent: [100] texture converges from both 0.7 and 1.4 to the same March–Dollase basin (~0.659), lowering the screened Rwp to 7.58%. Its [100] size branch also improves independently. I’m letting the matched alternatives finish, then I’ll test these effects jointly and from independent complete-model restarts.

The completed cubic screen confirms its earlier problem: its best apparent gains come from bound-hitting or basin-dependent broadening/texture, while benign texture axes return essentially unity. That makes the cubic model useful as a near-tied high-symmetry alternative, but not a defensible final source if the P2₁ result remains reproducible.

The P2₁ texture result is not confined to one crystallographic direction: [001] also gives a strong physical basin (screened Rwp 7.43%, MD ≈1.58). I’ll compare [100] and [001] under fully re-equilibrated models rather than combining multiple texture axes, since a single March–Dollase direction is the defensible low-flexibility description here.

The matched mechanism screen is complete. Neither P2₁/n parent becomes competitive; their only notable texture fits require extreme ratios (~3–6) and still leave Rwp near 9.8–10.8%. The final comparison is now between cubic-size, tetragonal-size, and three P2₁ descriptions (texture, size, and their joint model), each being restarted twice with the full nuisance model.

The first complete-model restarts preserve the source ordering: cubic-size Rwp 8.551%, tetragonal-size 8.509%, and P2₁ with one [001] texture term 7.293%. The high-symmetry results reproduce essentially digit-for-digit from independent size starts; I’m waiting for the second P2₁ texture restart and the size/joint branches before fixing the final profile model.

The P2₁ [001] size model is highly reproducible across independent starts (Rwp 6.837%, phase fractions agree within 0.006 wt%, candidate dimensions ~112 and 5.56 nm). Adding one [001] texture term gives a smaller further improvement (Rwp 6.802%, MD ~1.16); I’m checking whether its second restart reproduces the same curve and fractions before retaining that extra parameter.

The joint P2₁ profile model is reproducible: both restarts give Rwp 6.802%, the three fractions agree within 0.003 wt%, and the cells/profile parameters are effectively identical. I’m retaining the mild texture plus anisotropic size description and moving to same-composition source swaps for scheelite CaWO₄ and monoclinic WO₃, followed by phase-deletion tests.

I caught a staging bug before using the source-swap results: the restart preserved the P2₁ texture flag but reset its ratio to 1.0. I’m discarding those unmatched runs and rerunning with the established P2₁ and WO₃ texture values preserved; no submitted model has been affected.

The source normalization check found why one alternate behaved nonsensically: oxidation-decorated CIF atom labels were imported by GSAS-II as hydrogen. I normalized every serious source to elemental species and verified composition, symmetry, density, and minimum contacts first. The malformed 1931 CaWO₄ entry cannot be parsed and is excluded; the swaps are now restarting from normalized CIFs.

The corrected source swaps are now genuinely refining with the original space-group settings and elemental scattering factors intact. I’m treating the short 1.575 Å contact in COD 1528915 as a physical-review disadvantage, so it would need a clear, restart-stable fit advantage over the neutron-derived COD 2311041 source to displace it.

These swaps are slower because the lower-symmetry alternatives generate much larger reflection lists. The projects are still advancing (not hung); I’m keeping coordinates fixed and will only carry a source into texture testing if its no-texture baseline remains competitive.

The lower-symmetry source runs have stopped making useful progress—likely pathological cell/scale excursions during the simultaneous restart. Per the stated protocol, I’m treating that as inconclusive, stopping them, and restaging sequentially from scales-only with source cells fixed before allowing any cell/profile freedom.

The restaged scale-only source comparisons are decisive: triclinic WO₃ 11.58%, orthorhombic WO₃ 14.24%, the small-cell high-pressure WO₃ 15.78%, the older monoclinic source about 17.4%, and high-pressure fergusonite CaWO₄ 48.14%, versus 6.802% for the selected sources. None is close enough for added texture or cell freedom to be a credible tie.

Deletion testing confirms the third phase is not a profile artifact: removing Ca₃WO₆-type raises Rwp from 6.802% to 10.896%; removing WO₃ raises it to 15.851%, after complete re-equilibration. The CaWO₄ deletion is still converging, but its dominant peak family makes a similarly decisive result expected.

The stabilized inventory changes the earlier CaO verdict: with the P2₁ phase modeled correctly, CaO now refines to a plausible a ≈4.792 Å, ~86 nm size, and 1.52 wt%, lowering Rwp from 6.802% to 6.700%. I’m waiting for the independent high-scale restart and will retain it only if the same cell/fraction and coherent CaO line family recur.

CaO reproduces exactly from low and high scale starts. Its strongest 37.39° line improves dramatically, with additional consistent gains at 32.22°, 64.34°, and 67.58°; some weaker lines are overpredicted, so the ongoing CaO size/strain/texture screen will decide whether the assignment has a coherent physical intensity/profile description. The bronze remains a much more marginal ~0.28 wt% possibility.

CaO’s flexible branches are not trustworthy: uniaxial size hits the 1 nm bound, and texture solutions are start-dependent with 99.98% scale correlation and fractions ranging from ~0.1% to 1.0%. The simple isotropic CaO model is therefore the stable fraction estimate; the remaining symmetry-distinct axes are being checked for completeness.

The CaO mechanism pass is complete: every apparent improvement either hit a size bound, produced implausible strain, or changed the CaO fraction strongly across texture starts. I’m keeping isotropic size and no CaO strain/texture. The bronze [100] texture branch is the only trace-phase mechanism still under scrutiny because its first basin gave a notable improvement.

The bronze [100] texture term remains substantial after full re-equilibration: the first restart reaches Rwp 6.515%, MD ≈5.47, and ~0.55 wt% bronze. Because scale and texture are highly correlated and the orientation is strong, I’m waiting for the independent restart; reproducibility of the calculated curve and fraction—not the individual coefficient alone—is the acceptance criterion.

The bronze texture result reproduces across full restarts: Rwp 6.515%, bronze 0.548 vs 0.547 wt%, MD 5.47 vs 5.48, and nearly identical cells/curves. I’m retaining that correlated but predictively stable subspace. With the profile model complete, I’m now testing atomic-coordinate blocks separately; trace-phase coordinates will remain at source values unless unusually strong, physical, repeatable evidence appears.

The coordinate-block refinements are running in parallel for CaWO₄ oxygen, WO₃ tungsten/oxygen, and Ca₃WO₆-type W/Ca/O. Each branch starts with coordinates plus scales only, then re-equilibrates the complete profile model; this prevents a coordinate block from “winning” merely because nuisance terms were frozen.

CaWO₄ oxygen refinement is the first defensible atomic result: O moves by only a few hundredths of an ångström and Rwp falls from 6.515% to 6.460%. I will repeat that block from the independent nuisance start; it is not yet accepted solely on this run.

WO₃ W and Ca₃WO₆-type W blocks both improve the fit with modest-to-moderate shifts; WO₃ moves are only ~0.05–0.08 Å, while the Ca₃WO₆-type W shift reaches ~0.19 Å and needs stricter bond screening. Oxygen and Ca blocks are still completing.

The Ca₃WO₆-type Ca block lowers Rwp to 6.441% but moves some Ca sites by roughly 0.2–0.3 Å, so fit improvement alone is insufficient. The oxygen blocks are improving more strongly; they will be accepted only if bond/contact screening and independent restarts remain stable.

The all-oxygen blocks are rejected: Ca₃WO₆-type O sites moved by up to ~1.6 Å, and WO₃ O sites by several tenths of an ångström. Those gains are not physically defensible. I’m repeating the smaller CaWO₄-O, WO₃-W, Ca₃WO₆-W, and Ca₃WO₆-Ca blocks from the independent nuisance start before combining any of them.

Physical screening separates the coordinate results cleanly: CaWO₄-O, WO₃-W, and Ca₃WO₆-W remain chemically reasonable; the O-block fits create 0.97–1.42 Å W–O contacts and are rejected. The Ca₃WO₆ Ca block creates a 1.95 Å Ca–O contact, so I am also rejecting it despite its lower Rwp. I’m now combining only the three passing blocks in two independent chains.

The phase inventory is now well constrained: scheelite CaWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, a monoclinic Ca₃WO₆-type phase, plus small CaO and textured calcium tungsten bronze components. I’m combining the independently reproducible coordinate refinements, then I’ll rerun deletion/physical screens and produce a replayable GSAS-II project and exports.

The accepted coordinate changes are reproducing closely from two nuisance/profile starts. CaWO₄ oxygen and WO₃ tungsten shifts remain chemically sound; the broader oxygen and Ca-site trials were rejected because they created implausibly short contacts despite improving Rwp. The final cumulative Ca₃WO₆ tungsten check is still running.

All five current structures pass contact and coordination screening. The trace-phase deletion tests are now re-equilibrating the complete background, displacement, width, size, and texture model; this is the last inventory decision before export.

The final inventory survives deletion: removing CaO raises Rwp from 6.376% to 6.443%, and removing the bronze raises it to 6.563%. Both trace scales remain positive and significant after full re-equilibration, so I’m retaining them while labeling their structural/fractional uncertainty explicitly.

The submitted model now passes the final project audit: all scales are positive, no variables are frozen at bounds, the background stays positive, ADPs/occupancies are physical, all CIFs parse, and the observed profile in `fit.csv` exactly matches the public input. An independent `final.gpx` replay changes Rwp by only 0.00018 percentage points.

